CHAPTER I
INTRODUCTION
Introduction
In today’s digital era, students are constantly surrounded by advanced technology, yet many remain unaware of one of the most pressing environmental problems: soil pollution (John & B, 2023). Unlike widely discussed issues such as climate change, soil pollution often receives little attention despite its major impact on food safety, human health, and ecosystem stability. This gap reveals the difference between what students should know and what they actually understand. Traditional lessons rarely explore the issue in depth, and existing learning materials are limited, leaving students with an incomplete grasp of its causes, effects, and possible solutions.
To help address this gap, the EcoWay mobile application was developed as an interactive tool designed to make learning about soil pollution both engaging and enjoyable. The app incorporates multimedia content, quizzes, and interactive activities to support student participation and improve knowledge retention. This study examines how the EcoWay application enhances students’ understanding by comparing pre-test and post-test results, ultimately evaluating its effectiveness as an educational tool. It aims to determine whether EcoWay can meaningfully improve Senior High School students’ comprehension of soil pollution at STI College Dasmariñas and to explore the broader potential of mobile technology in environmental education.


This study focuses on assessing the effectiveness of the EcoWay mobile application in improving Senior High School students’ knowledge of soil pollution at STI College Dasmariñas. The respondents are limited to selected Senior High School students enrolled at STI College Dasmariñas during the academic year in which the research is conducted. The study measures students’ knowledge before and after using the app through quantitative assessments and gathers their perceptions and experiences through qualitative methods. The research is limited to soil pollution concepts included in the EcoWay application and does not cover other environmental topics.
However, the study has several limitations. The EcoWay mobile application is not available online and can only be accessed through mobile devices, restricting its use to students who own compatible phones. The research is also confined to one institution, which means the findings may not be applicable to other schools or learning environments. In addition, the study measures knowledge gains only within the research period and does not assess long-term retention or possible changes in students’ environmental behavior.






Background of the Study
In today’s generation, students are surrounded by technology. Mobile phones, applications, and online platforms are part of their daily routines. Despite this exposure to technology, many students still lack sufficient knowledge about important environmental issues, especially soil pollution. While topics such as climate change and water pollution are commonly discussed, soil pollution often receives less attention in classroom discussions. However, soil pollution is a serious problem because it affects food safety, human health, crop production, and the overall balance of the environment.
Environmental literacy plays an important role in helping students understand these issues and develop responsible behavior. Studies have shown that structured environmental lessons can improve students’ knowledge and awareness. For example, the study conducted by Vasquez et al. (2021) revealed that students showed improvement in environmental literacy after participating in organized learning activities. This suggests that when environmental topics are properly integrated into lessons, students are more likely to understand and appreciate their importance.
In addition, technology-based learning has been found to positively influence students’ engagement and understanding. According to Fulgencio, P. J. and Marcia, A. (2025), students reported high levels of engagement and usefulness when using digital learning tools. This shows that learners are open to using technology as part of their educational experience. When lessons are delivered through interactive platforms, students may find them more interesting and easier to understand.
Similarly, research by Seifi, Z. et al. (2025) demonstrated that a mobile application used as an educational intervention significantly improved users’ knowledge and practices related to environmental issues. Their findings support the idea that mobile applications can serve as effective tools for delivering environmental education. Another study by Mahsun et al. (2025) showed that students who participated in interactive and project-based learning activities gained higher environmental awareness compared to those who experienced traditional teaching methods. These studies highlight the importance of using innovative and engaging strategies in teaching environmental topics.
With this in mind, the EcoWay mobile application was developed as an interactive learning tool focused on soil pollution. The application provides lessons, visual materials, and interactive content designed to help students better understand the causes, effects, and prevention of soil pollution. By using a pre-test and post-test design, this study aims to measure whether the EcoWay mobile app can significantly improve the knowledge of Senior High School students at STI College Dasmariñas.
This study is important because it explores how technology can be used to address gaps in environmental knowledge. By determining the effectiveness of the EcoWay mobile app, the research hopes to contribute to improving environmental education and promoting greater awareness of soil pollution among students.












