Research Questions
      This study  aims  to determine the Effectiveness of EcoWay Mobile Application on Enhancing Student’s Knowledge of Soil Pollution  among Senior High School Students.
Particularly, the study seeks to answer the following questions:
How effective is the EcoWay mobile application in increasing students’ understanding of soil pollution concepts?
What is the level of students’ knowledge about soil pollution before using the EcoWay mobile application?
What are the factors affecting the EcoWay Mobile Application’s effectiveness in improving the Soil Pollution Knowledge  of Students in terms of: 
Graphics 
Interactivity 
User-Friendly Interface 


Hypothesis 
Null Hypothesis (H0) :
 EcoWay, a Mobile Application, does not significantly enhance students’ knowledge about Soil Pollution of Senior High School students specifically at STI College Dasmariñas.
There is no significant relationship between the Interactive Elements, and Content Accuracy in enhancing students' knowledge, and application of the topic 


Significance of the Study 
 	This study is important since it evaluates how effective the EcoWay Mobile Application is in improving Senior High School students’ knowledge about soil pollution. The findings may benefit the following:
Environmental Organization. This research may support environmental organizations in finding ways that effectively enhance knowledge and inspire actions among youth individuals. Demonstrating digital tools like EcoWay can serve as a tool to become a future model for future Mobile Application’ creation that promotes Healthy Soil Environment.
Teachers. The result of this research can help teachers understand how interactive tools and platforms like EcoWay can make learning about Soil pollution more interesting and engaging for students. By using these kinds of tools, teachers can create a more enjoyable and effective way of teaching the topic.
Students. This study will help students gain a deeper knowledge of Soil Pollution, its causes, and ways to prevent it. By engaging with the Application, students may find it more engaging and enjoyable to use, leading to better application of the topic in students’ academic everyday life.
App Developers. This research will provide insight into how a mobile application can be used as a creative tool for learning. Web developers, especially those working on educational and environmental projects, can use the results as a guide to improve their app designs. 
Future Researchers. This study will act as a reference for future researchers who are interested in examining similar topics involving Soil pollution. Especially in designing and evaluating the effectiveness of a mobile application aimed at improving awareness and understanding of its causes and consequences.


Definition of Terms
Operational Definition
EcoWay Mobile Application – The interactive digital platform designed to deliver lessons, quizzes, and simulations on environmental topics for learners.


Interactive Learning Modules – Structured digital content within the app that combines visuals, exercises, and multimedia to engage students.


User Interface – The layout and navigational design of the app, measured by ease of access and usability feedback.


Gamification Elements – Features such as points, badges, and interactive games used to motivate and engage learners.


Environmental Literacy – The ability of learners to recognize, understand, and evaluate environmental issues in daily life.


Digital Engagement – The level of active participation of learners with the mobile application’s features.


Content Accuracy – The correctness and reliability of information provided within the EcoWay modules.


Multimedia Resources – Audio, video, graphics, and text integrated into the app to support learning.


Cognitive Retention – Learners’ ability to recall information after interacting with the mobile application.


Visual Learning Aids – Graphical and video materials used to enhance understanding of environmental concepts.


Motivational Feedback – Messages and prompts within the app that encourage continued learning and engagement.


Self-Paced Learning – The ability of learners to progress through app content at their own speed.


Interactive Exercises – Tasks within the application designed for hands-on engagement with environmental topics.


Lesson Completion Rate – The proportion of modules completed by learners during the intervention period.


Learning Outcomes – Measurable changes in learners’ comprehension and application of environmental concepts.


Educational Technology – Digital tools and platforms, like mobile apps, used to facilitate teaching and learning.


Survey Instrument – A questionnaire designed to collect learners’ perceptions, experiences, and feedback.


Digital Accessibility – The extent to which learners can easily access and navigate the mobile application.


Curricular Integration – The incorporation of environmental topics into the school’s existing curriculum.


Student Perception – Learners’ attitudes and opinions toward the EcoWay mobile application and its features.


Instructional Design – The planned structure and organization of app content to facilitate learning.


Assessment Items – Questions or tasks within the app or survey used to evaluate learner performance.


Knowledge Retention Rate – The degree to which learners maintain understanding of concepts over time.


Engagement Metrics – Quantitative indicators of learners’ interaction with app activities (e.g., time spent, modules completed).


Digital Pedagogy – Teaching methods that leverage digital tools to enhance learning experiences.


Technological Adoption – The extent to which learners embrace and effectively use the mobile application.


Learning Analytics – Data collected from learners’ interactions with the app to measure performance and engagement.


Cognitive Gains – Improvements in learners’ understanding and ability to apply environmental concepts.


Interactive Assessment – App-based tasks that allow learners to receive immediate feedback on their performance.


Instructional Efficacy – The effectiveness of the app in achieving its educational objectives.


User Experience – Learners’ overall satisfaction and comfort while using the mobile application.


Module Interactivity – The degree to which learners engage actively with content rather than passively consuming it.


Environmental Awareness – Learners’ consciousness and understanding of environmental challenges in their community.


Learning Motivation – The internal drive of learners to complete modules and engage with app content.


Digital Learning Platform – A comprehensive technology-based system, like EcoWay, used to deliver educational content and measure learning outcomes.


          






Conceptual Framework

                                                 Figure 1 : Conceptual Framework
 
 
Figure 1 illustrates the conceptual framework guiding this study on how the EcoWay Mobile Application serves as an effective educational platform in enhancing the students’ knowledge on soil pollution. The conceptual framework begins with the Senior High School students of STI College Dasmariñas, which is the target audience of this study. Next is the data gathering; Senior Highschool students respondents take a pre-test to measure the respondents’ existing knowledge regarding soil pollution. After taking the pre-test, participants will use the EcoWay mobile application, made to enhance knowledge on soil pollution. As the students finish using the EcoWay mobile application, the respondents take a post-test to determine if there is any change in respondents’ knowledge. Lastly, the survey made by researchers will be answered by respondents and used as a basis for measuring the effectiveness of the EcoWay mobile application  in enhancing students’ knowledge on soil pollution. 
 












