Global  
The Role of Mobile App  in Promoting Sustainable Environmental Practices
A study titled “The effect of an educational intervention based on a mobile application on women’s knowledge, attitudes, and practices with respect to microplastics and health: a randomized controlled trial” examines how a mobile learning app can influence users’ environmental knowledge, attitudes, and their daily practices. Since mobile devices are already part of their everyday life, they offer a good way to deliver short, accessible lessons about sustainability, such as waste reduction, consumption responsibly, and simple eco-friendly habits (Seifi et al., 2025).
This study supports this present research because both highlight the importance of interactive digital tools in environmental education. Both studies demonstrate that digital platforms can enhance environmental learning by relating it to what students usually do everyday; using mobile phone, and by providing clearer visual representations of environmental topics.



Comparison of Experimental and Control Group in the Environmental Awareness
A study by Mahsun et al. (2025) titled “Enhancing Environmental Awareness: Evaluating the Impact of Project‑Based Hybrid Learning on Critical Thinking for High School Students” examined how a blended project‑based learning model influenced students’ understanding of environmental issues. In the research, high school students from a school in Indonesia were divided into an experimental group, which experienced Project‑Based Hybrid Learning (PJBHL), they focused on creating products from recycled materials, and a control group which followed traditional teaching methods. Pre-intervention and post‑intervention assessments measured students’ “environmental awareness” and critical thinking skills. Based on results, the results showed that  students in the experimental group improved significantly in their environmental awareness compared to those in the control group, suggesting that the hybrid project‑based approach can effectively deepen students’ environmental knowledge.

The results of the study demonstrated the effectiveness of project-based approaches in enhancing students’ knowledge about the environment, more than those who do not. This study serves as our foundation and evidence that measuring the effectiveness of the Eco Way that we will use in enhancing the student's knowledge is important. Although it is not the same as using mobile apps, Both studies focus on measuring the effectiveness of our interventions in enhancing students’ understanding of soil pollution.




Exploring the potential of smartphone applications as educational tools in the Ecology teaching–learning process
A study titled “The Potential of Using Smartphone Applications as Educational Tools” examined the use of mobile apps for ecology teaching and learning in Brazilian basic education. The researchers identified and evaluated multiple mobile applications focused on ecological topics, assessing their technical quality, interactivity, and overall educational effectiveness (Ueno & Silva, 2025). The study highlighted that well-designed applications with strong technical reliability and interactive features significantly enhance students’ engagement and understanding of environmental concepts. It also emphasized that accessibility to learning materials and user-friendly design are critical for ensuring that students can effectively navigate and benefit from mobile educational platforms.
This study supports the present research on the EcoWay mobile application for senior high school students, as it underscores the importance of app design features—such as interactivity, technical performance, and accessibility—in improving learning outcomes. Both studies demonstrate that providing students with interactive, easily navigable, and content-rich learning modules can enhance their comprehension of environmental topics. In the context of EcoWay, these findings reinforce the potential for mobile apps to deliver engaging lessons on soil pollution, allowing students to access information conveniently while actively participating in the learning process. The study strengthens the rationale for designing EcoWay as an interactive and technically reliable platform to support environmental education.


Development of Mobile Learning Application System for Environmental Science Material (SARITHA Apps)
An international study from Indonesia (Prasetyo et al., 2024) developed and evaluated a mobile learning application specifically designed for environmental science content, aiming to improve students’ understanding and cognitive skills in environmental topics. The research employed quantitative methods, using pre-test and post-test assessments to measure the effectiveness of the application in enhancing learners’ knowledge. The results demonstrated that the mobile learning tool significantly improved students’ cognitive skills and comprehension of environmental science concepts, illustrating the potential of mobile applications to support meaningful learning in this field. By combining structured content with interactive features, the study highlighted how mobile apps can make complex environmental topics more accessible and engaging for students.
This study supports the rationale behind EcoWay, suggesting that similar mobile applications can serve as effective tools for increasing environmental knowledge. In particular, it reinforces the potential for apps like EcoWay to improve understanding of specific topics such as soil pollution among senior high school students. By leveraging interactive content, assessments, and engaging educational design, EcoWay can help students develop stronger cognitive skills and deeper awareness of ecological issues, demonstrating the value of mobile learning platforms in promoting both knowledge acquisition and environmental literacy.


The Use of Augmented Reality-Supported Activities in Environmental Education for Early Childhood: A Quasi-Experimental Study

. The results showed that children who experienced AR activities displayed higher engagement levels and achieved better understanding of environmental concepts compared to those who learned through traditional methods. The combination of interactive elements and enhanced visual content helped students understand abstract environmental concepts through real world environmental examples.
The research establishes technological learning methods as essential for environmental education. Although this study used AR and involved younger learners, it demonstrates that interactive visual tools can make environmental concepts, like pollution more understandable. An approach that can be adapted in mobile applications for senior high school students. 

BeeLife: A Mobile Application to Foster Environmental Awareness in Classroom Settings

A recent study (2024) explored the use of a mobile application to enhance students’ environmental knowledge and attitudes. The research found that students who engaged with the application demonstrated greater environmental awareness and increased motivation to adopt sustainable behaviors compared to those who received traditional classroom instruction. The app incorporated game elements alongside interactive materials, which encouraged higher student participation, promoted active learning, and improved long-term retention of environmental knowledge. By combining engagement, interactivity, and gamified learning strategies, the study highlighted the effectiveness of mobile applications in making environmental education more impactful and memorable for students.
This study supports the present research by demonstrating how mobile applications can effectively enhance environmental learning outcomes. Both studies emphasize that interactive and engaging experiences help students develop a deeper understanding of ecological topics. By providing visually appealing content, interactive exercises, and gamified elements, mobile apps like EcoWay create opportunities for students to gain a more meaningful understanding of specific environmental issues, such as soil pollution. This approach ensures that learning is not only educational but also motivating, accessible, and relevant, reinforcing the potential of mobile applications as powerful tools for environmental awareness and knowledge development.

The Potential of Using Smartphone Applications as Educational Tools in Ecology Teaching–Learning Processes

Ueno and Silva (2021) evaluated smartphone applications specifically designed for ecology education by assessing multiple factors, including content quality, interactivity, and technical performance. Their study concluded that well-designed applications significantly enhance students’ conceptual understanding while also increasing engagement with environmental topics. The research highlighted that interactive features, clear content organization, and responsive app functionality are essential elements for effective learning, showing that students benefit most when digital tools are thoughtfully designed to support both comprehension and active participation. This study underscores the importance of aligning educational technology with pedagogical principles to maximize learning outcomes in environmental education.
This research strongly supports the objectives of the EcoWay project, as both focus on leveraging mobile applications to teach environmental topics. It emphasizes that the quality of app design and the inclusion of interactive features are critical factors in improving student knowledge. For EcoWay, these findings reinforce the value of prioritizing user-friendly interfaces, engaging simulations, and interactive exercises, all of which contribute to a deeper understanding of soil pollution and related ecological concepts. By integrating these elements, EcoWay aligns with proven strategies for effective mobile-based environmental education, demonstrating the potential to enhance both learning outcomes and student engagement.


The Effects of Integrating Mobile Devices with Teaching and Learning on Students’ Learning Performance: A Meta-Analysis

Sung, Chang, and Liu (2022) conducted a comprehensive meta-analysis examining the impact of mobile learning across a wide range of educational studies. Their findings demonstrated that mobile-based learning significantly improves academic performance, particularly when applications incorporate multimedia elements, interactive exercises, and user-friendly interfaces. The study emphasized that engagement through interactivity and visual content is a key factor in enhancing learning outcomes, showing that students respond more positively to platforms that allow them to actively participate in the learning process. These results highlight the effectiveness of mobile learning as a global educational strategy, underscoring its potential to support diverse learning needs and contexts.
This global meta-analysis provides strong theoretical support for the EcoWay project. It confirms that interactive mobile learning tools are effective in enhancing student knowledge and engagement, reinforcing the idea that a well-designed application can improve understanding of complex topics. In the context of EcoWay, this evidence suggests that features such as quizzes, visual simulations, and interactive lessons can facilitate deeper comprehension of soil pollution, making learning both meaningful and engaging for senior high school students. The study strengthens the rationale for using mobile platforms as a powerful educational tool in environmental science education.







The Influence of Smartphone Educational Applications on Secondary School Science Achievement

Alrasheedi and Capretz (2024) explored the impact of educational applications on science achievement in secondary schools across an international context. Their study revealed that students who used educational apps not only demonstrated higher academic performance but also developed more positive attitudes toward science. By integrating interactive exercises, assessments, and multimedia content, these applications helped students engage more deeply with scientific concepts, promoting both understanding and retention. The research highlights the potential of mobile technology to transform traditional science education into a more interactive and motivating experience, showing that students respond positively to platforms that make learning both accessible and engaging.
This study supports the objectives of the EcoWay project, particularly its goal of improving science-related knowledge in environmental education. It reinforces the argument that smartphone-based learning can enhance academic outcomes, providing empirical evidence that well-designed mobile applications can strengthen both conceptual understanding and interest in scientific topics. For EcoWay, these findings suggest that incorporating interactive lessons, quizzes, and other app-based features can effectively improve students’ knowledge of soil pollution while fostering a more engaging and motivating learning environment.



The Impact of Mobile Applications on Environmental Sustainability Learning: Evidence from Secondary Education

Ahmed, Lee, and Thompson (2025) investigated the use of mobile applications as tools for teaching environmental sustainability. Their study found that incorporating interactive mobile apps into the curriculum significantly improved students’ environmental literacy, while also raising awareness of sustainability issues among secondary students. By providing engaging content, such as visual simulations, quizzes, and interactive lessons, these applications helped students connect theoretical concepts to real-world environmental challenges. The study highlights the potential of mobile technology to make learning more accessible, interactive, and impactful, especially in topics that benefit from experiential and visual learning approaches.
This research closely aligns with the objectives of the EcoWay project, given its focus on environmental education. It reinforces the notion that mobile applications can be highly effective in increasing knowledge and awareness of ecological issues, including soil pollution. For EcoWay, this suggests that features such as interactive simulations, gamified lessons, and immediate feedback can not only enhance understanding of soil pollution but also cultivate a sense of environmental responsibility among learners. The study provides empirical support for the use of mobile platforms as educational tools that combine engagement, interactivity, and knowledge development in environmental science.



Local 
Environmental Literacy of Senior High School Students in Selected Philippine Colleges
A study titled “Environmental and Disaster Risk Reduction literacy among Senior High School students in Sibagat Agusan del Sur” explored environmental and disaster risk reduction literacy of senior high school students in Sibagat, Agusan del Sur, Philippines. Using a surveyed sample from several strands such as STEM, GAS, Agriculture, EIM, the study measured students’ awareness of environmental issues that contribute to disaster risk and their practices toward disaster risk reduction. The results showed generally high levels of environmental and disaster risk reduction literacy, with students showing strong awareness of environmental concerns and actively engaging in disaster preparedness and “mitigation”. Awareness levels varied significantly across academic strands.. The findings highlight the role of formal education, curricular integration, and information sources, such as social media, in shaping environmental literacy among Senior High School students, and recommend strengthening environmental education and school programs to further enhance students’ knowledge (Bernal & Apdohan, 2023).
Their study showed that structured environmental learning activities increase students' environmental literacy, aligning with the purpose of the Eco Way mobile app. Both studies show the importance of access to learning materials to enhance students' understanding and promote better actions for the environment.






Students' Perception on Using Technology for Learning Environmental Topics
A study from Fulgencio and Marcia (2025) titled “Level of Use of Technology Learning Tools and Learning Outcomes among Junior and Senior High School Learners” collected data from students in a public school, using surveys to measure learners’ views on the accessibility, engagement, and usefulness of digital tools such as educational apps, devices, and online platforms. The finding showed that learners reported “very high” perceptions of engagement and usefulness and “high perceived” accessibility of technology learning tools. This suggests that Filipino students feel positively about integrating technology into their learning experiences, which can be a good foundation for exploring how technology influences learning in specific subjects, including environmental topics like soil pollution.

This study supports the development of the Eco Way mobile app because both emphasize the benefits of integrating technology into the learning environment. Although their study focuses on specific subjects, positive student perceptions toward technology indicate that digital tools can be beneficial Similarly, the Eco Way mobile app aims to use an interactive and visually engaging online platform to help students understand soil pollution concepts more effectively. By applying technology as the main medium for instruction, Eco Way aligns with the findings that students could learn better and show greater interest when digital resources are used.






Effectiveness of Mobile Phones as a Learning Aid among Senior High School Students
This study examined the impact of mobile phones on students’ academic outcomes and mobile-assisted learning experiences in Senior High School settings. The findings demonstrated that the use of mobile technology significantly supported students in their learning processes, reinforcing the idea that mobile applications can enhance subject understanding, knowledge retention, and overall academic performance at the senior high level (San Pedro Relocation Center National High School et al., 2023). By providing access to interactive and flexible learning materials, mobile-assisted learning helped students engage more deeply with the content, promoted self-paced learning, and allowed for continuous feedback, all of which contributed to more effective educational outcomes.
The study supports the present research by suggesting that similar applications, such as EcoWay, could have a positive impact on students’ knowledge of specific topics like soil pollution. Both studies highlight the importance of interactive digital tools in education, emphasizing that well-designed mobile applications can make learning more engaging and meaningful. For EcoWay, this underscores the value of features such as quizzes, visual simulations, and interactive exercises, which can help senior high school students develop a deeper understanding of environmental issues while reinforcing key concepts through active participation.




Assessing the Effectiveness of Mobile Learning Apps in Enhancing Student Learning and Engagement in Selected Secondary Public Schools in General Trias City” 
The study found that interactive applications incorporating quizzes and gamified lessons enabled students to gain a deeper understanding of educational content, while also contributing to increased class attendance rates. The integration of game-like elements and interactive exercises made learning more engaging and motivating, encouraging students to participate actively in their lessons. Moreover, students who already owned smartphones could conveniently access lessons anytime and anywhere, highlighting the accessibility advantage of mobile learning platforms. By providing flexible and stimulating learning materials, these applications helped bridge the gap between traditional classroom instruction and modern digital learning needs, fostering both academic performance and student engagement.
This research supports the objectives of the EcoWay study, as both investigations emphasize the role of mobile applications in enhancing knowledge acquisition. Just as the study demonstrated that interactive mobile tools improved learning outcomes in general educational subjects, it suggests that a similarly designed application focused on soil pollution can help senior high school students develop a stronger understanding of environmental topics. By leveraging features such as quizzes, interactive exercises, and gamified content, EcoWay can provide an engaging platform that encourages active learning, reinforces conceptual understanding, and supports students in acquiring meaningful knowledge about soil pollution and its broader ecological implications.

 I READ: Interactive Reading Mobile Application for Enhanced Senior High School Reading and Writing Skills"
The results of the study demonstrated that students who utilized the application achieved higher post-test scores, reflecting notable improvements in their reading comprehension and writing abilities. The application’s interactive design, which incorporated exercises and provided instant feedback, created a dynamic learning environment where students could engage with educational content effectively while also enjoying the process. This approach not only enhanced academic skills but also encouraged active participation, fostering a sense of motivation and confidence in learners as they navigated through their tasks. By blending education with interactive engagement, the study illustrates how well-designed mobile applications can transform learning into an enjoyable and productive experience.
This research supports the objectives of the EcoWay study by showing that customized, interactive mobile applications especially those designed by universities or educational institutions for specific study programs—can significantly enhance student learning outcomes. For EcoWay, the use of interactive features and immediate feedback mechanisms is particularly relevant, as these elements help students build a deeper understanding of soil pollution and related environmental issues. By providing an engaging platform that encourages exploration and reinforces knowledge through practice, EcoWay mirrors the proven benefits of interactive learning applications, demonstrating the potential for mobile technology to improve both comprehension and practical understanding in environmental education.


 Effectiveness of Mobile Learning Applications in Improving Science Achievement Among Secondary Students
Dela Cruz and Mendoza (2022) conducted a study investigating the effectiveness of mobile learning applications in enhancing science performance among Filipino high school students. Employing a quasi-experimental research design, the researchers compared students who used mobile-based instructional materials with those who received traditional classroom instruction. The study revealed that students who engaged with mobile learning tools showed a significant improvement in post-test scores, demonstrating that interactive and accessible digital resources can positively impact academic performance. These results highlight the potential of technology-enhanced learning in making complex scientific concepts more understandable and engaging for students, particularly in a context where traditional methods may not fully capture learners’ attention.
The findings of this study strongly support the rationale behind the EcoWay project, as both investigations focus on measuring knowledge improvement through structured assessments. By providing empirical evidence that mobile applications can significantly enhance science-related knowledge, this research aligns closely with EcoWay’s objective of improving understanding of soil pollution. Moreover, it underscores the value of incorporating mobile technology into educational strategies, reinforcing the importance of EcoWay’s interactive features and user-friendly design in facilitating effective learning experiences. This connection highlights that well-designed mobile applications can serve as powerful tools for promoting environmental literacy and supporting meaningful knowledge acquisition among learners.

Mobile App Usability and Student Engagement in Environmental Science Education
Santos, Ramos, and Villanueva (2023) conducted a comprehensive study examining the usability and effectiveness of mobile applications in environmental science courses. Their research highlighted that applications which integrate multimedia elements, interactive quizzes, and visual simulations significantly enhance student engagement. In addition, these features were shown to improve learners’ conceptual understanding of complex ecological topics, such as ecosystem dynamics, biodiversity, and pollution processes. The study underscores the critical role that well-designed digital tools play in facilitating active learning and promoting deeper comprehension in environmental education.
This research is particularly relevant to the EcoWay platform, as both initiatives focus on leveraging mobile technology to support environmental education. By emphasizing interactive and user-friendly app design, the study reinforces the notion that thoughtfully structured digital tools can positively influence learning outcomes. For EcoWay, this connection highlights the importance of its features—such as engaging simulations, quizzes, and visual content—in helping users develop a stronger understanding of soil pollution and related environmental issues. Overall, the findings provide empirical support for the design principles underlying EcoWay, suggesting that the platform’s approach can effectively enhance knowledge retention and foster greater environmental awareness among learners.



Evaluating the Effectiveness of Mobile Environmental Educational Games on High School Learners
Garcia and Alonzo (2025) conducted a study assessing the effectiveness of a mobile-based environmental educational game developed for high school students. Using a mixed-method research design, the researchers combined quantitative data from pre-test and post-test assessments with qualitative feedback gathered through student interviews and surveys. The results revealed a significant increase in students’ post-test scores after interacting with the mobile application, indicating improved understanding of environmental concepts. Additionally, students reported high levels of engagement, motivation, and satisfaction with the app’s interactive features, multimedia elements, and user-friendly interface. The study concluded that well-designed mobile educational games can effectively support environmental learning by making complex topics more accessible and engaging for secondary learners.
This research directly supports the present EcoWay study, as both investigations focus on evaluating mobile applications specifically designed to teach environmental concepts to secondary-level students. Similar to Garcia and Alonzo’s study, the EcoWay mobile application integrates interactive modules and assessment features aimed at enhancing students’ knowledge—in this case, focusing on soil pollution. The findings of Garcia and Alonzo reinforce the assumption that interactive and engaging mobile learning tools can significantly improve students’ environmental knowledge and overall learning experience. Furthermore, their use of a mixed-method approach aligns with the methodology of the present study, strengthening the theoretical and empirical foundation for assessing EcoWay’s effectiveness among Senior High School students.

Development and Validation of E-SelfIMo: E-Learning Self-Directed Interactive Module in Earth Science 
             	This research focused on creating and validating E-learning Self-directed Interactive Modules (E-SelfIMo) for Earth Science using the Borg and Gall development framework. Eight e-modules were developed through Kotobee software, evaluated by both experts and students, and tested for their effectiveness in improving conceptual understanding. The modules were confirmed by experts to meet DepEd’s standards for non-printed learning resources, while students rated them highly in terms of content, design, and usefulness. Pretest and posttest results showed that learners who used the modules advanced from “Near Mastery” to “Near Full Mastery” of the topics, demonstrating greater improvement compared to the control group. The study concluded that E-SelfIMo promotes interactive, independent, and engaging learning, which enhances students’ motivation, interest, and performance, and can serve as an effective technology-based teaching tool (Lasala Jr., 2023). 
             	This is directly connected to our study on the Air-ducate website because both highlight how digital learning platforms can effectively improve student learning and awareness. Just as E-SelfIMo enhanced Earth Science understanding through interactive modules,Air-ducate aims to increase senior high school students’ awareness of pollution control by providing an engaging, technology-driven platform. Both studies emphasize that integrating technology into education not only supports self-directed learning but also enhances student engagement and long-term retention of knowledge. 




Environmental and Disaster Risk Reduction literacy among Senior High School students in Sibagat Agusan del Sur 
            	This study investigated the level of environmental and disaster risk reduction literacy among Senior High School students in Sibagat, Agusan del Sur, using a modified questionnaire across different academic strands. Findings revealed that social media was the most common source of information, while earthquakes and floods were the most frequently experienced hazards, with early evacuation being the main coping strategy. Overall, students showed high awareness of environmental issues linked to disaster risk and demonstrated strong disaster risk reduction practices. A negative correlation was found between awareness and the Electrical Installation and Maintenance (EIM) strand, while practices showed a strong positive correlation with the Agricultural strand but a negative one with the EIM strand—both statistically significant. The study recommended incorporating environmental and disaster risk reduction into the school curriculum, strengthening related programs, and institutionalizing eco-movements through student organizations (Bernal, 2023). 
         	This relates to our Air-ducate website study as both highlight the importance of educational tools in raising environmental awareness and responsible practices among students. While the Sibagat study emphasized the curriculum and school-based programs, Air-ducate  introduces a digital approach to pollution control awareness, offering students a modern and accessible platform for learning that can complement and enhance traditional educational methods. 


Synthesis
           The reviewed literature consistently emphasizes the importance of digital learning platforms in enhancing students’ understanding of environmental issues, particularly air pollution. Studies show that technology-based instructional tools such as educational websites, interactive modules, and multimedia resources significantly improve knowledge retention, engagement, and conceptual understanding among learners. Research also highlights that visually enriched and user-friendly online learning environments help simplify complex scientific ideas, making them more accessible to senior high school students.
           Existing findings further reveal that soil pollution education is most effective when delivered through platforms that encourage self-paced exploration, interactive learning, and immediate feedback features commonly found in modern digital learning systems. Several studies affirm that exposure to online educational content leads to measurable gains in environmental awareness and promotes responsible behavior toward pollution prevention and control. 
          In connection to the present study, the literature supports the premise that the EcoWay Mobile Application can serve as an effective instructional tool for improving students’ Soil pollution knowledge. Since prior studies confirm the positive impact of digital learning platforms, it is reasonable to expect that the website’s multimedia lessons, quizzes, and interactive features will similarly enhance the learning outcomes of STI College – Dasmariñas students. Therefore, the reviewed literature collectively strengthens the assumption that using technology-driven educational interventions such as EcoWay Mobile Application  can significantly improve students’ understanding of environmental concepts, as measured through pre-test and post-test results.






CHAPTER II
METHODOLOGY
Research Design
According to Lis-Prince (2025), experimental research design stands as the “standard approach” for studying cause‑and‑effect relationships, whereby the researcher manipulates one or more independent variables and measures the outcomes on dependent variables. This study will utilize a quantitative one-group pretest-posttest design within an experimental framework to measure. the effectiveness of EcoWay mobile app and and to enhance students’ knowledge of soil pollution at STI College Dasmariñas Senior High School” for parallel structure.. Pre-tests and post-tests were administered to measure changes in students’ understanding of soil pollution. The results will be analyzed using descriptive statistics and paired t-tests to determine whether the EcoWay mobile application significantly improves students’ knowledge and its practical application.. 

Ethical practices, including informed consent, confidentiality, and voluntary participation, will be strictly observed. This study aims to demonstrate, through analysis of the collected data, how technology-based platforms how technology-based platforms like the EcoWay mobile application can effectively support students with limited knowledge of soil pollution.




Population and Sample
         The respondents of our study will  be  all Senior High School students in STI College- Dasmariñas for the S.Y 2025-2026 with a total population of 192 Senior High School Students. The sample size was determined to be 130 students after calculating using the Slovin’s formula. The selected respondents were from TVL and Academic tracks offered at STI College-Deamariñas, involving: ICT-MAWD ( Information and Communication Technology - Mobile App and Web Development), ICT-CCT ( Information and Communication Technology - Computer and Communication Technology),  HETO ( Home Economics - Tourism Operations), HECA ( Home Economics - Culinary Arts), ABM ( Accountancy, Business, and Management), and HUMSS ( Humanities and Social Sciences) .

Sampling Design
 This research will use  a simple random sampling procedure in selecting its participants. The 130 respondents were all Senior High School students from STI College-Dasmariñas who will be chosen because they represent the target group for the topic. The research will focus on finding out how the EcoWay Mobile Application will improve the students’ knowledge about Soil pollution s. Since Senior High School students are at a stage where they are still developing their values and habits, it is expected that they will show a significant level of environmental knowledge that can still be strengthened through online learning. By selecting this group of students, the study will be able to evaluate how effective the EcoWay mobile application will be in providing information, shaping attitudes, and encouraging positive practices related to pollution control. In this way, the research will also show the potential of online learning platforms in helping young people become more responsible and knowledgeable about the environment. 
        
Research Instrument
The study will make use of a pre-test, survey questionnaire, and post-test and the EcoWay mobile application to measure its  effectiveness in enhancing students’ soil pollution knowledge. The test consists of 15 multiple-choice items administered before and after the intervention to measure changes in students’ knowledge of soil pollution, including its definition, consequences, and ways to prevent it. The survey questionnaire employs a four-point Likert scale (1 = Highly Ineffective, 2 = Ineffective, 3 = Effective, 4 = Highly Effective) to capture students’ perceptions of the app and their learning experience.
All test and survey questionnaire items will be reviewed by a statistician, and only validated and improved questions will be included in the final instrument to help ensure alignment with the study’s objectives. The EcoWay mobile application will serve as the main intervention by providing lessons, interactive content, and educational materials aimed at enhancing students’ knowledge of soil pollution, allowing the researchers to measure its overall effectiveness through the comparison of pre-test and post-test results.






Data Gathering Procedure
 After the research instrument was approved for use in gathering the necessary data, the researchers submitted a formal letter to the school authorities seeking permission to conduct the study among Senior High School students. This approval guaranteed that all research activities complied with the institution’s guidelines and ethical standards. Once the respondents were identified, the researchers designed a survey questionnaire to evaluate their feedback on the effectiveness of the Ecoway Mobile  application. The questionnaire was then reviewed and validated by instructors to confirm its accuracy, relevance, and clarity before being administered to the students, who accomplished it after completing both the pre-test and post-test.
The researchers then administered a researcher-made pre-test and post-test, which were validated by instructors to ensure they accurately measured students’ knowledge of Soil Pollution Awareness. The pre-test contained multiple-choice items aimed at evaluating the respondents’ prior knowledge before their exposure to the EcoWayMobile  application, with the results recorded as baseline data for comparison. Following this, participants were given access to the EcoWay Mobile  application and allowed to explore its various features. Each student was provided sufficient time to go through the interactive sections available on the platform.
After the application learning session, the respondents completed a post-test administered one day later. This assessment included the same 15 multiple-choice questions as the pre-test, but the items were rearranged to maintain fairness. The researchers then recorded the post-test results to evaluate whether there were improvements in the students’ knowledge before and after using the EcoWay Mobile  application.

Data Process and Statistical Treatment
  The data gathered from Senior High School students will be organized, encoded, and checked to ensure accuracy and reliability. Each completed questionnaire will be reviewed for completeness before being entered into a spreadsheet. After encoding, the responses will be tabulated and processed according to the variables of the study, which include factors influencing the mobile app’s effectiveness in terms of user interface, interactive elements, and content accuracy. To ensure that the number of respondents is appropriate and representative, the researchers will use Slovin’s Formula to determine the sample size:
					
